deep.reconstruction:
This page comes from Grothendieck's preparatory notes for the Nov.–Dec. 1967 IHES lectures on crystals (later written up by Coates–Jussila as 'Crystals and the de Rham cohomology of schemes', Dix exposés 1968). It continues point c) of §1, a survey of what is missing from algebraic de Rham cohomology over C.

(1) What already works. For X smooth and proper over a field of characteristic 0, the usual duality formalism holds for H*_DR(X) = H*(X, Ω•_X):
- the cup-product pairing H^i × H^{2n−i} → H^{2n} ≅ k is perfect (Poincaré self-duality);
- this gives Gysin maps;
- there is a cohomology class cl(Z) ∈ H^{2q}_DR(X) for a cycle of codimension q;
- these are compatible with direct images, inverse images and intersection products.
Grothendieck says all of this is done 'quite analogously' to what he did for Hodge cohomology ⊕ H^q(X, Ω^p). This must refer to his Bourbaki exposé 149 (1957) on coherent duality, where cycle classes in H^q(X, Ω^q) and their compatibilities are treated, or to the related Chern-class work.

(2) The limitation. Such a duality is confined to proper X. A reader who knows the f^!, f_! (six-operation) formalism will see how restrictive this is.

(3) What he wants. A relative formalism Rf_!, f^! for arbitrary separated morphisms of finite type of schemes, of the kind developed in Hartshorne's Residues and Duality (LNM 20, 1966), but with coefficients that include de Rham complexes. A first draft restricting to 'almost proper' maps is crossed out, as is a phrase about coefficients in de Rham complexes.

(4) Expected payoff. Such a formalism should automatically give an algebraic description of singular homology, i.e. de Rham homology defined via f^! of the base. He says this is suggested elsewhere in his 'papiers bleus'.

(5) Technical requirement. Duality theory should be extended from complexes with O-linear differentials (the coherent complexes of Residues and Duality) to complexes whose differentials are differential operators, the de Rham type.

The next page concludes: points b) and c) both amount to finding the right category of coefficients. That category should contain both coherent complexes and de Rham-type complexes; it leads to the crystals and D-module viewpoint. In modern terms this is a six-functor formalism for algebraic de Rham cohomology. It was realized later by:
- Herrera–Lieberman (1971) and Hartshorne, 'On the de Rham cohomology of algebraic varieties' (1975), giving algebraic de Rham homology and duality for non-proper schemes;
- the holonomic D-module six operations (Bernstein, Kashiwara, Mebkhout, late 1970s–80s);
- in characteristic p, Berthelot's crystalline duality and arithmetic D-modules.
